        teacherNotes: "Key questions 2: 'Why do angles NOT change during enlargement or reduction?'Expected answer: 「Angles do NOT change because enlargement and reduction create SIMILAR figures. Similar figures have the same SHAPE (angles) but may have different SIZE (side lengths). Angles define the shape. If angles changed, the shape would change (no longer similar). For example: a square has four 90° angles. If we enlarge a square by k = 3, the image is still a square (same shape), so all angles are still 90° (not 90° × 3 = 270°). The sides get longer (×3), but the angles stay the same (90°). Scale factor k applies to LINEAR dimensions (lengths), not to ANGULAR measures (angles). Enlargement/reduction: same shape (angles equal), different size (lengths proportional). So angles do NOT scale. They stay equal.'(Ifchildcan't answer, guide: 'Enlargement/reduction 创造相似图形. Similar figures: same shape (angles equal), different size (lengths proportional). Shape 由 angles 决定, not能变. Lengths can变（×k), angles not能变（保持相wait）. ')",
