        teacherNotes: "Ask the child: 'Today we learn median of ungrouped data（one组未分组数据中位数）. Let's review what 'median' means. Median is a measure of central tendency (集中趋势测量值). Median = the middle value when the data are arranged in order（中位数 = 数据排序后中间值）. Important: The data must be arranged in order first. Step 1: Arrange the data in order from smallest to largest（从小到大排序）. Example: data set 7, 2, 9, 5, 12. Unordered. First, arrange in order: 2, 5, 7, 9, 12. Now the data are in order. Step 2: Find the middle. Count how many values: n = 5 (five values). Odd number. The middle position is (5 + 1) ÷ 2 = 3rd position. The 3rd value is 7. Median = 7. Important: The median is 7, not 9 (the unordered middle position before sorting), not 12 (the largest value), not the mean, not the mode. The median is the middle value after ordering. Common fossils: (1) using the unordered middle position (forgot to order the data first; took the middle position before ordering); (2) using the largest value as the median (confused median with maximum); (3) using the mean instead of the median (confused median with mean; Week 88 taught mean, this week teaches median; they are different measures of central tendency); (4) using the mode instead of the median (confused median with mode; Week 89 taught mode, this week teaches median; they are different). Remember: median = the middle value after ordering. Always order the data first, then find the middle.'",
